ucturally plausible samples. 1 Introduction Score-based diffusion models , originally inspired by thermodynamic principles ( Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015 ) , have emerged as a powerful class of generative models, achieving state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of applications ( Song and Ermon, 2019 ; Ho et al., 2020 ; Song et al., 2021a ; Rombach et al., 2022 ; Saharia et al., 2022 ; Watson et al., 2023 ; Abramson et al., 2024 ) . At a high level, diffusion models generate samples by progressively denoising a sequence of intermediate distributions { P i } i = 1 N \{P_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N} , thereby transforming a simple, easy-to-sample distribution P 1 P_{1} (e.g., Gaussian noise) into a complex target distribution P N P_{N} . This generative procedure is driven by a sequence of score functions { ∇ log ⁡ P i } i = 1 N − 1 \{
