that the resulting dynamics inherently adapt to the intrinsic geometry of the target distribution. In practice, however, diffusion guidance can still produce physically implausible samples, particularly when the guidance strength γ \gamma is large. Our results suggest that this behavior may stem either from errors in score estimation or from an insufficiently fine discretization scheme. Note that diffusion models are generally expected to fail under inaccurate score estimation or coarse discretization. 1.2 Related works Theoretical explanations for the success of diffusion guidance. A recent line of work seeks to theoretically explain the empirical success of diffusion guidance. Among others, Wu et al. (2024a) analyzes diffusion guidance when the target distribution is a Gaussian mixture model (GMM). Specifically, they show that applying guidance to both DDIM and DDPM increases classification confidence, defined as the posterior probability of the label of interest. Moreover, they prove that for guided DDIM, diffusion guidance decreases differential entropy, theoretically validating the empirical observation that guidance reduces sample diversity. Chidambaram et al. (2024) analyzes guided DDIM applied to GMM or mixture distributions supported on disjoint intervals. Their results show that strong guidance steers the generated samples toward the boundary of the target component. More recently, several works have studied diffusion guidance for general distributions without an explicit parametric form ( Bradley and Nakkiran, 2024 ; Li and Jiao, 2025 ; Jiao et al., 2025 ; Guo et al., 2026 ) . Since we have discussed these works earlier, we omit further discussion here. Reward-guided diffusion models. Another application of controllable generation with diffusion models is reward-guided diffusion ( Fan et al., 2023 ; Yuan et al., 2023 ; Black et al., 2024 ; Wallace et al., 2024 ; Gao et al., 2024 ; Jiao et al., 2025 ) , where the objective is to generate samples that achieve higher reward values. These methods modify the reverse diffusion dynamics by incorporating gradients of a reward function, thereby steering the generation process toward outputs that receive higher reward. Here, the reward function might represent various objectives, including alignment with human preferences or adherence to given constraints. We refer the reader to Uehara et al. (2025) for a review of recent developments in this area. Other theoretical advances for diffusion models. Various results have been established to explain the overall success of diffusion models, covering topics including convergence rates ( Lee et al., 2023 ; Chen et al., 2023 ; Benton et al., 2024 ; Li et al., 2024a ; Wu et al., 2024a ; Gupta et al., 2025 ; Li et al., 2025b ; Li and Yan, 2025 ; Huang et al., 2025a ; Huang et al., 2025b ; Li et al., 2025a ) , score estimation ( Oko et al., 2023 ; Wibisono et al., 2024 ; Zhang et al., 2024 ; Dou et al., 2024 ) , and applications in statistical or optimization settings ( Montanari and Wu, 2023 ; Chung et al., 2023 ; Li et al., 2024b ; Chen et al., 2025 ) . We refer interested readers to Chen et al. (2024) for a summary of recent theoretical developments in the past few years. 1.3 Organization In Section 2 , we provide a brief overview of diffusion models and diffusion guidance. In Section 3 , we present and prove the main results for DDIM, while Section 4 presents the corresponding results for DDPM. We provide intuition for our theoretical results in the main text, while deferring the complete proofs to the appendix. Numerical experiments are reported in Section 5 . We conclude the paper and discuss several directions for future work in Section 6 . 1.4 Notation For d ∈ ℕ + d\in\mathbb{N}_{+} , we define the set [ d ] = { 1 , 2 , ⋯ , d } [d]=\{1,2,\cdots,d\} . For a probability measure μ \mu over ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , we denote by supp ⁡ ( μ ) \mathrm{supp}(\mu) the support of μ \mu . Unless otherwise specified, the symbol ‖ ⋅ ‖ \left\|\cdo