cretized approximations of these continuous-time processes. Diffusion models through a continuous-time perspective. A common choice for the forward process is based on the Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process. Specifically, let p 0 p_{0} denote the target distribution to be sampled. We then consider the following stochastic differential equation (SDE) defined on 0 ≤ t ≤ T 0\leq t\leq T : d ​ y t = − y t ​ d ​ t + 2 ​ d ​ W t , y 0 ∼ p 0 , \displaystyle\mathrm{d}y_{t}=-y_{t}\,\mathrm{d}t+\sqrt{2}\,\mathrm{d}W_{t},\qquad y_{0}\sim p_{0}, (1) where ( W t ) t ≥ 0 (W_{t})_{t\geq 0} denotes a standard ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} -valued Wiener process that is independent of y 0 y_{0} . For 0 ≤ t ≤ T 0\leq t\leq T , we denote by p t p_{t} the distribution of y t y_{t} . Standard computation implies that p t ​ = 𝑑 ​ λ t ​ y 0 + σ t ​ w , ( y 0 , w ) ∼ p 0 ⊗ π d , λ t = e − t , σ t = 1 − λ t 2 , \displaystyle egin{split}&p_{t}\overset{d}{=}\lambda_{t}\,y_{0}+\sigma_{t}\,w,\qquad(y_{0},w)\sim p_{0}\otimes\pi_{d},\ &\lambda_{t}=e^{-t},\qquad\sigma_{t}=\sqrt{1-\lambda_{t}^{2}},\end{split} (2) where π d \pi_{d} denotes the distribution of a standard Gaussian random vector in ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} . From Eq. 2 , we see that process ( 1 ) begins with the target distribution, and progressively injects Gaussian noise until the resulting distribution approaches a standard Gaussian one. For the reverse process, two commonly used approaches are DDIM and DDPM. Their continuous-time formulations corresponding to the forward process ( 1 ) are given below: • DDIM sampler. The continuous-time process underlying the deterministic DDIM sampler is formulated as an ordinary differential equation (ODE) on 0 ≤ t < T 0\leq t<T : d ​ x t = ( x t + ∇ log ⁡ p T − t ​ ( x t ) ) ​ d ​ t , x 0 ∼ p T . \displaystyle\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\left(x_{t}+
